In non-commutative group-based cryptography one designs cryptographic protocols (key-exchange, encryption, digital signatures, etc.) whose security rests on the algorithmic difficulty of certain problems in a non-abelian group G, called the platform group. Typical hard problems considered include the conjugacy search problem (given g, h = x^{-1} g x in G, find a conjugator x), the word problem, the membership problem, the simultaneous conjugacy search problem, the decomposition problem, and variants thereof. A platform group G is considered suitable if it satisfies, at least, the following informal requirements:
  (i) elements of G admit a unique, efficiently computable normal form, so that the word problem in G is solvable in (low) polynomial time;
  (ii) the group law and inversion are computable in polynomial time on normal forms;
  (iii) there exist efficient algorithms to sample elements from G according to distributions usable in protocols;
  (iv) the underlying hard problem (e.g. conjugacy search) has no known subexponential-time algorithm on G, and known length-based, linear-algebraic, or quotient attacks fail;
  (v) keys and ciphertexts admit succinct representations (polynomial in the security parameter).

Identify and characterize a class C of finitely generated non-abelian groups satisfying conditions (i)–(v) above and which provides the most appropriate platform for non-commutative group-based cryptographic schemes. More precisely: produce a class C of groups together with explicit normal-form and sampling algorithms, and a candidate hard problem P on groups in C, such that P is provably (or convincingly) resistant to all currently known generic attacks (length-based attacks, linear/representation attacks, quotient attacks, subgroup attacks), while still supporting the construction of secure key-exchange and public-key encryption protocols.

